WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.700
No matter how many awards an actor wins, they'll
always have a few movies under their belt

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.800
that they might not be so proud of.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.960
From cheesy action movies to overly dramatic
bio-pics, sometimes an actor can't help but

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.170
cross the line into insane territory, often
with amazing results.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:21.050
And in some movies, it'd be ridiculous not
to give 110 percent.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.790
Are these the worst examples of overacting
of all time...or the best?

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:26.790
You decide.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:30.570
Either way, they're all inanely fun to watch.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:31.670
Con Air

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.510
Playing a really bad guy is tricky business.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:39.680
Go too far, and the whole endeavor winds up
coming across as laughably cartoonish.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.289
If there's one actor who can unnerve an audience
as a villain, it's John Malkovich.

00:00:44.289 --> 00:00:49.149
He's played memorably chilling creeps several
times, such as his Oscar-nominated role as

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:52.409
a would-be presidential assassin in In the
Line of Fire.

00:00:52.409 --> 00:00:54.510
It went a bit differently with Con Air.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:59.559
In this action drama about a mutiny on a prisoner
transport plane, Malkovich plays a master

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:03.850
criminal who pushes his insanity to its amazing
limits.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.229
"Make a move and the bunny gets it."

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:08.229
The Room

00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:11.210
At this point, The Room is practically legendary.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:16.460
It's so filled with inept performances, bizarre
acting choices, and completely unnecessary

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.659
scenes that it has to be seen to be believed.

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:19.659
"That's me.

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:20.659
How much is it?"

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:21.659
"It'll be eighteen dollars."

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:22.659
"Here you go.

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:23.659
Keep the change."

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:24.659
"Hi doggie."

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:25.659
"You're my favorite customer."

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:26.659
"Thanks a lot, bye!"

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:29.990
That's all primarily the fault of Tommy Wiseau,
who wrote, directed, and produced The Room

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:34.850
and also stars as Johnny, a San Francisco
finance guy caught up in a doomed love triangle.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.539
"You're lying, I never hit you.

00:01:37.539 --> 00:01:39.990
You're tearing me apart, Lisa!"

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:45.670
Keep in mind that in all these scenes, the
writer, director, producer, and actor all

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.069
agree that the performances are spot-on,because
they're all the same person.

00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:53.149
"One of them found out about it, beat her
up so bad about it she ended up in a hospital

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:54.149
on Guerrero Street."

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:55.680
(weird laugh)

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.860
"What a story, Mark."

00:01:57.329 --> 00:01:58.560
Spawn

00:01:58.569 --> 00:02:02.040
It's hard to blame John Leguizamo for his
scenes in Spawn.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.039
In the film, he plays Violator, a character
whose every move is ridiculous.

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:07.979
"Spawny Spawny he's our man.

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:11.090
If he can't kill them no one can.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.090
Yayyyyy Spawny"

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:16.800
The character's obviously supposed to be unpleasant,
but in the film he makes clowns even more

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.739
unpleasant than they usually are.

00:02:18.739 --> 00:02:24.030
But let's be clear here: this is an evil clown
demon in a cheesy, over-the-top '90s comic

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.030
book movie.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.590
Anything less from Leguizamo would have been
under-acting.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.900
"Oh come on you scream like a girl, do it
like this…"

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.720
(screams)

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:34.900
Mommie Dearest

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.239
There are a few amazing things about Faye
Dunaway's over-the-top performance in Mommie

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:41.239
Dearest.

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:46.489
For starters, she was playing Joan Crawford,
a real-life Golden Age Hollywood actress.

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:51.290
Dunaway's portrayal of Crawford as a self-absorbed,
violent monster ultimately meant that the

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:55.500
would-be drama has since come to be regarded
as a camp comedy classic.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.129
The most famous scene sees Dunaway truly heading
into the stratosphere, Crawford really hates

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:05.400
wire hangers, and when she finds one in her
daughter's closet, well, she's a little more

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.060
than just disappointed.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:09.660
"Why?

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.020
Whyyyy!

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.659
Christina get out of that bed!"

00:03:15.659 --> 00:03:17.290
Spider-Man 3

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.690
Sam Raimi's Spider-Man 3 gets a bit of a bad
rap, but for a good reason.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:26.719
After two stellar films featuring everyone's
favorite web-slinger, Raimi introduces some

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:31.690
ill-advised plotlines, one of which renders
Peter Parker completely unlikeable.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:35.959
On the bright side, that plot gives us some
of the most memorable Spidey moments of all

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:39.299
time, when the Venom symbiote changes Peter's
personality.

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:43.280
Tobey Maguire goes all in on Peter's bad boy
montage.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.750
It's one of the most unintentionally hilarious
parts of the film, or any superhero film for

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:48.750
that matter.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:53.049
Peter sports a "hip" black outfit and rhythmically
shuffles down the street, flashing finger

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:55.079
guns at pretty women.

00:03:55.079 --> 00:04:03.060
(still better than The Amazing Spider-Man
2)

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.680
He fails miserably, and it's all kinds of
fun to watch.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.890
Maguire is hamming it up more than any other
actor in the trilogy, clearly aware of how

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.500
ridiculous the story is to begin with.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.920
"This is for you…"

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:23.160
Spider-Man 3 marks the end of Raimi's time
with the character, but thanks to Maguire's

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.440
ridiculous performance, it goes out on an
unforgettable note.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.080
Power Rangers

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:33.540
Considering it's based on a television series
that's at its best when the camp and excess

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.670
is working in overdrive, 2017's Power Rangers
film did a pretty good job of reimagining

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.910
the beloved team of teenagers.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:45.690
While it never forgets to be fun, it makes
every effort to tell a grounded and more human

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:46.690
story.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:50.140
One character seems to be constantly striking
against the grain, though.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:54.820
It's not clear if Elizabeth Banks, the actress
behind the villainous Rita Repulsa, got the

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:56.830
"grounded and more human" memo.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.850
Her take on the space witch is way over the
top.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:05.520
Although …yeah, it's kind of understandable
considering she's playing a space witch.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.960
"Make my monster grow!!"

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:17.220
You can't say it isn't entertaining, it's
just a different kind of entertaining than

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.380
the rest of the film.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:22.030
There's certainly a place for performances
like this, but even in the context of a Power

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:26.200
Rangers film it's some seriously laughable
stuff.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.690
The Fifth Element

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:33.820
In the '90s, actor Gary Oldman came into his
own as a veritable high prince of overacting.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:38.840
Films like True Romance and Leon: The Professional
feature Oldman channeling an amazing level

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.340
of energy into these more villainous roles.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.660
"Bring me everyone."

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.800
"What do you mean everyone?"

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.700
"Everyone!!!!!"

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:53.140
But nothing can match the overacted hysteria
of his performance in The Fifth Element.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.720
Oldman plays the film's antagonist, Zorg.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:01.270
Before he even starts overacting, you can't
help but laugh thanks to his ridiculous haircut

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:04.050
and hyper-stylized Southern accent.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:07.870
"It's light; handle's adjustable for easy
carrying; good for righties and lefties; breaks

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:12.400
down into four parts; undetectable by X-ray;
ideal for quick discreet interventions."

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.160
Pair that with a truly bonkers performance
and you've got one of the most peculiar entries

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:18.190
in Oldman's canon.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:19.190
"Look it's easy.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:20.300
Look at my fingers.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.300
Four stones.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:21.960
Four crates.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.160
Zero stones.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.140
Zero crates!"

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.550
Oldman has since reeled it in quite a bit
and even scored best actor at the 2018 Oscars

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:35.180
for Darkest Hour, but it would be nice to
see him in a Zorg-style role again someday.

